<h2>The Send-Engine Request — Overview, Scope, Interface</h2>
<p>AI Notification is complete up to deciding "what · when · to whom · through which channel" to send and producing the <strong>send request</strong>. We ask the Platform team to implement a <strong>Kafka (message queue) based send engine</strong> that will take over everything after that — the <strong>actual sending, timing, reprocessing, and status</strong>.</p>

<div class="note"><span class="nh">Purpose of this document</span>
This document requests that the sending stage be implemented as a shared send engine. It is organized conceptually around <strong>what the Platform team needs to build</strong> and <strong>what AI Notification needs</strong>; it does not contain a concrete internal design (details to be settled through discussion).</div>

<h3>Overview</h3>
<p>The AI Notification system takes business events and user requests, applies rules to decide <strong>which notification · at what moment · to whom · through which channel</strong> to send, and keeps the result in the form of a <strong>"send request"</strong>. That is the AI Notification scope completed in the first implementation phase.</p>
<p>What is needed now is a <strong>send engine</strong> that takes those send requests and <strong>actually sends them, executes the timing at the right moment, reprocesses failures, and manages and displays the send status</strong>. Since high volume, asynchronous processing, delivery guarantees, and scalability are required, we ask that it be implemented on Kafka.</p>

