<h3>Send Engine Implementation Requirements</h3>
<p>Each item describes, at a conceptual level, "what the engine must do and why". The concrete methods and structure are decided by the Platform team's judgement and through discussion.</p>

<h4>Receiving send requests · channel sending</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Receiving send requests</strong> — take send requests from the queue and process them.</li>
  <li><strong>Channel sending</strong> — send through notification (in-app), note, and mail, and it must be possible to add channels such as <strong>chat and KakaoTalk in future</strong>. Per-channel rules (what the engine must honour): mail needs a subject, each channel differs in whether a given recipient is reachable, and some channels allow a message to be recalled after it is sent.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Reliability — idempotency · delivery guarantee · ordering · reprocessing</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Idempotency / duplicate prevention</strong> — even if the same send request arrives twice, it is actually sent only once.</li>
  <li><strong>Delivery guarantee</strong> — send without loss and, where possible, confirm and report back <strong>whether it actually arrived (and was read)</strong>. (Today confirmation after "it was sent" is weak, so this is a key improvement.)</li>
  <li><strong>Ordering guarantee</strong> — preserve the send order for the same target and within the same request.</li>
  <li><strong>Retry · backoff · DLQ</strong> — retry transient failures automatically; isolate repeated failures in a dead-letter queue for the operator to <strong>reprocess manually</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Scheduling · cancel/recall</strong> — scheduled sending, cancelling before the send and recalling after the send (only on channels that support it).</li>
  <li><strong>Bulk processing · parallelism</strong> — send to large recipient lists in parallel, meeting throughput and latency targets.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Sending by timing — details · implementation request</h4>
<p>"When to send" differs by type. AI Notification decides the <strong>timing type and its parameters</strong> and carries them in the send request; the engine must then <strong>actually execute the timing</strong> that matches that type. (Not a vague "send as soon as a request arrives" — the behaviour must be implemented per type.)</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Timing type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What the engine must execute</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Immediate</strong></td><td>Send as soon as the event occurs</td><td>Send without delay (real time)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Delayed</strong></td><td>Send when a given state persists for a set time (e.g. 3 days waiting for approval)</td><td><strong>Wait</strong> until the scheduled time, then send (for finalizing at the moment of sending, see below)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Recurring</strong></td><td>Repeat on a fixed cycle or date/time (e.g. daily at 09:00, the 5th of each month)</td><td><strong>Run the recurring schedule</strong> + prevent duplicate sends + batch lifecycle</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Transition</strong></td><td>Send <strong>the moment</strong> a state changes to a specific value (e.g. sales order confirmed)</td><td>The <strong>detection is done by AI Notification</strong> → immediately send the request handed over at the moment of transition (the engine does not observe business state)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
