<h4>Latest lookup and finalization at send time</h4>
<div class="note"><span class="nh">Important</span>
Sending must use <strong>the latest data at the moment it is actually sent, not the values held when the send request was created</strong>. Because of the delayed, recurring, and transition types there is a time gap between creating the request and actually sending it, and the target data can change in between. A step is therefore needed that, at the moment of sending, <strong>looks up and finalizes again "who to send to and what to send right now"</strong>.</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Finalization (lookup and resolution) is done by AI Notification</strong> (interpreting business data and recipients is AI Notification's area).</li>
  <li>When the timing arrives, the engine <strong>asks AI Notification to finalize "the final targets and content for this send"</strong> and sends based on that result. (If the target has disappeared by then, that item is excluded from sending.)</li>
  <li>This "finalize at send time" procedure must be part of the interface.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Dynamic lifecycle management of recurring batches</h4>
<p>Recurring (scheduled) notifications are not a fixed batch: <strong>batches must come into existence and disappear</strong> as notifications are registered and removed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Creation</strong> — when a recurring notification is <strong>registered</strong>, <strong>dynamically create</strong> the matching schedule batch.</li>
  <li><strong>Removal</strong> — when that notification is <strong>deactivated, deleted, or past its end date</strong>, <strong>automatically remove and release</strong> the batch.</li>
  <li>Purpose: keep unused batches from piling up. (Cycle = ‘how often it repeats’; lifecycle = ‘when that repeating batch is created and removed’.)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Send data management · status (display)</h4>
<p>Not only the sending itself, but also <strong>management of the send data</strong> and a <strong>status screen</strong> for operators are needed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data management</strong> — retention and cleanup of send request, processing result, and history data.</li>
  <li><strong>Status screen / dashboard</strong> — distribution of processing states (waiting · in progress · success · partial failure · failure), retry and DLQ status, statistics by channel and by company, <strong>looking up failed items and reprocessing them manually</strong>, looking up and cancelling scheduled items.</li>
  <li>Operators must be able to check "what was sent, when, and how" and act on it immediately.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Observability · multi-tenancy · security</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Observability · operations</strong> — monitoring metrics such as throughput and failure rate, failure alerts, reprocessing tools.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-tenancy</strong> — it must be <strong>isolated per company</strong>, and no path such as an all-company bulk send may be opened.</li>
  <li><strong>Security</strong> — safe management of channel credentials, protection of recipients' personal data.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Interface · Scale · Alignment · Expected Benefits</h3>

<h4>Interface — request to finalize it first (top priority)</h4>
<div class="note"><span class="nh">Priority request</span>
Implementing the whole engine takes time, so we ask that the <strong>interface (the specification of what is exchanged) be finalized and provided before the engine itself</strong>. Only then can AI Notification <strong>implement in parallel</strong> the parts that publish send requests and receive results.</div>
<h4>Proposed order of work</h4>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Present the preferred option</strong> — we prefer the direction in which AI Notification <strong>publishes</strong> send requests to the queue and the engine consumes them.</li>
  <li><strong>Agree on the direction</strong> — discuss this direction at <strong>the highest priority</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Finalize the interface first</strong> — as soon as we agree, lock the interface down first → start parallel development.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Interfaces needed (direction · information exchanged)</h4>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Interface</th><th>Direction</th><th>Information exchanged (conceptual)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>①</td><td>Send request delivery</td><td>AI Notification → engine</td><td>Request identifier, company, send target, message content, recipient list, channel, <strong>timing type and parameters</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>②</td><td>Finalization at send time (lookup · resolution)</td><td>Engine → AI Notification → engine</td><td>When the timing arrives, request finalization of "the final targets and content to send now" → the finalized result is returned</td></tr>
    <tr><td>③</td><td>Send result · status return</td><td>Engine → AI Notification</td><td>Request mapping, success/partial failure/failure per channel, failure reason, delivered · read</td></tr>
    <tr><td>④</td><td>(Optional) status · history integration</td><td>To be discussed</td><td>How the status screen links to AI Notification data if the engine provides it</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="scn-sub">The concrete fields and formats are settled through discussion. This document presents only the direction and the kinds of information exchanged.</p>

<h4>Scale · SLA · priority</h4>
<p><strong>Scale and SLA</strong> (daily/peak send volume, the delay allowed for "immediate", history retention period, granularity of result returns) are <strong>settled through discussion</strong>. The minimum scale (order of magnitude) needed for effort estimation is decided together at the kickoff.</p>
<h4>Priority (phases)</h4>
<p><strong>Phase 1 · essential</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Notification, note, and mail sending + idempotency and delivery guarantee</li>
  <li>Timing for the four timing types + finalizing with the latest data at send time</li>
  <li>Recurring batch lifecycle management</li>
  <li>Result and status returns + the send status screen</li>
  <li><strong>Finalizing the interface first</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Later phases</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Advanced scheduling/recall features, expansion to the KakaoTalk and chat channels</li>
  <li>Read confirmation, advanced statistics and analytics</li>
</ul>

<h4>Items that need discussion</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The interface direction (preferred option → fast finalization)</li>
  <li>The real-time criterion for "immediate" sending (allowed delay)</li>
  <li>The order and priority of channel expansion</li>
  <li>The scope of delivery and read confirmation (how much is returned)</li>
  <li>The concrete integration for finalizing targets at send time (finalization request and response)</li>
  <li>Who holds and manages the channel credentials</li>
  <li>Rough figures for send volume and SLA (for effort estimation)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Expected benefits · schedule</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Delivery reliability</strong> — sending without loss or duplication, with delivery confirmation.</li>
  <li><strong>Scalability</strong> — coping with high volume, many channels, and new channels.</li>
  <li><strong>Operability</strong> — status views and reprocessing reduce the operational burden.</li>
  <li><strong>Shared asset</strong> — turning the send engine into a shared asset.</li>
</ul>
<p>We intend to <strong>start the discussion in early August</strong>, aim to <strong>finalize the interface in early August</strong> if possible, and then move on to implementing the send engine.</p>
